Chapter: KeysNumpad

  cites UI chapter KeyInput

 Proof that `poll-key` decodes the numpad against NumLock and the `0xE0`
 one-shot. `keys-mods` pinned the held/toggle modifiers; this pins the two
 the numpad needs: NumLock (a toggle on bit 4) and the `0xE0` prefix (a
 one-shot on bit 5 that marks the next key as the dedicated arrow).

 The `.keys` timeline: NumLock on (69), numpad-8 (72), numpad-minus (74),
 `0xE0` (224) then numpad-8 (72), NumLock off (69), numpad-8 (72), Enter.
 The four collected keys must be:

   56   -- NumLock on, bare 0x48 is the digit `8`
   45   -- numpad `-` is its symbol either way
   38   -- 0xE0 then 0x48 is the dedicated Up arrow, NumLock notwithstanding
   38   -- NumLock off, bare 0x48 is Up again

 A numpad that ignored NumLock gives 38 for the first; one that folded the
 0xE0 prefix into the digit gives 56 for the third. Only the two bits
 decoded correctly yield this.

Section: Definitions

  kn-collect : List Integer -> List Integer
  kn-collect (acc) =
    let ch = poll-key
    in if ch == 0 then kn-collect acc
    else if ch == key-enter then acc
    else kn-collect (list-push acc ch)

  kn-show : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Console] Integer
  kn-show (xs) (i) (len) =
    if i >= len then 0
    else act
      print-line-uni (integer-to-text (list-at xs i))
      kn-show xs (i + 1) len
    end

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    let ks = kn-collect []
    in act
      kn-show ks 0 (list-length ks)
      print-line-uni "numpad"
    end
  end
